1. Test Objectives

>The main objective is to make sure that users can securely and successfully log in to the food delivery application.

1)Requirement Analysis Stage: Verify that 100% of Login requirements such as valid login, invalid credentials, password rules, and account lockout are clearly understood and covered by test scenarios.
2)Test Case Design Stage: Prepare test cases covering at least 95% of identified Login requirements, including positive, negative, boundary, and validation scenarios.
3)Test Execution Stage: Execute 100% of planned Login test cases and achieve at least 95% pass rate before considering the module ready for release testing.
4)Defect Reporting/Retesting Stage: Ensure that 100% of Critical and Blocker Login defects are fixed and successfully retested before the test cycle is closed.

2. Scope of Testing

>>In-Scope
The following items will be tested during this cycle:
1)Login with valid and invalid username/email and password.
2)Field validations, including empty fields, incorrect formats, and password requirements.
3)Forgot Password functionality and password reset flow.
4)Login error messages and validation messages.
5)Login button, password visibility option, and basic UI behavior.
6)Session handling after successful login and logout.

>>Out-of-Scope
The following items will not be tested in this cycle:
1)Food ordering and checkout functionality after login.
2)Payment gateway and payment transactions.
3)Restaurant search, menu, cart, and delivery tracking features.
4)Detailed performance testing of the entire application.
5)Third-party authentication systems if they are outside the Login module's control.

3. Entry Criteria

Testing of the Login module can begin when:
-The Login requirements and acceptance criteria are approved and available to the QA team.
-A stable build containing the Login feature is deployed in the test environment.
-Required test accounts and test data are available.
-Test cases have been reviewed and approved.

4. Exit Criteria

-Testing can be completed when:
-100% of planned Login test cases have been executed.
-All Blocker and Critical defects are fixed and successfully retested.
-No unresolved defect exists that prevents users from logging in or accessing their accounts.
-The final test results and defect summary have been documented and shared with stakeholders.

5. Risks and Mitigation Strategies
Risk 1: Test environment or authentication server is unavailable
>Impact: Test execution may be delayed.
>Mitigation: Maintain a backup test environment and verify server availability before starting the test cycle.

Risk 2: Test accounts or login data are unavailable
>Impact: QA may not be able to test different login scenarios properly.
>Mitigation: Prepare valid, invalid, locked, and newly registered test accounts before test execution begins.

Risk 3: Critical Login defects are discovered just before release
>Impact: The release may be delayed or users may experience login failures.>
>Mitigation: Perform early smoke and functional testing as soon as the Login build is available and prioritize Critical/Blocker defects for immediate fixing.

6. Overall Test Approach

The Login module will be tested using functional, negative, validation, usability, compatibility, and basic security testing. Testing will start early in the STLC and continue through test execution, defect reporting, retesting, and final closure.

Expected outcome: The Login feature should allow authorized users to log in successfully, reject invalid credentials correctly, provide meaningful error messages, and handle login-related failures without affecting the user experience

